#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189114} :activity/id #uuid "4306f994-2576-5ea8-aa86-53b75b9d993b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050682 :topic/id #uuid "5e9ef58b-352e-5161-ad27-7cfe61e49ba0" :topic/math-academy-id 3863} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188209 :question/id #uuid "e4007ddd-a5ed-5f3d-96cc-4c911d97a615" :question/math-academy-id "e-9187"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188546}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"} :step/id #uuid "57744d65-db5a-5f3d-bf13-43a012006fdf" :step/math-academy-id 34269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188210 :question/id #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9188"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188561}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"} :step/id #uuid "36904357-d45f-58d8-af71-db1903bb3b44" :step/math-academy-id 34272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188002 :question/id #uuid "175f3917-8801-515d-a493-5f36a52aa01b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10097"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188563}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"} :step/id #uuid "9b164372-2e9b-5b11-9a7e-f7aa6897c577" :step/math-academy-id 38081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189379 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B)\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B$!\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y$, respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y$. Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "c151ba98-beac-55c8-9a33-b3f619f0a290" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "074fbae0-d9f5-5e1f-8944-2352a5ff16a7" :step/math-academy-id 38083 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189419 :tutorial/content "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If\n> $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)$\n> for some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.*\n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e6987fa-0133-5b85-b6a5-e58a401020b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6534 :tutorial/title "Independence When the Joint Support Is Rectangular"} :step/id #uuid "260c1a0b-a4ec-5618-9cb3-1bfc17d53a21" :step/math-academy-id 39479}] :activity/title "Independence of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188458}]}
